Choosing both sides at one exterior vertex
=========================================

This is an alternative traversal of the exact finite parameter space in
outside_route.rst, not a different normalization or a new graph premise.
Fix an exterior forest H and its normalized left subsets B_i. A completed
graph is specified uniquely by the pairs (B_i,C_i), where C_i is the subset
of right leaves adjacent to exterior vertex i. Each subset meets a rooted
branch at most once and |B_i|+|C_i|=4-deg_H(i). Every prescribed leaf capacity
and both sets of three root-pair totals must be filled. There are no other
unknown edges, since H was fixed at the start.

The separate traversal chooses all B_i before the C_i. The joint traversal
chooses B_i and C_i in turn for each exterior vertex. These orders enumerate
the same assignments. In particular the canonical conditions involve only
the B_i and allow ties; C_i is not compared to another right subset. Any
completed graph can first be transported by an automorphism of H to its
normalized B_i, and the resulting C_i are then among the unrestricted
choices of the joint traversal. There is no dependence on the original
label order of right incidences.

Every prune of the joint traversal is necessary. Available leaf capacities
must be nonnegative, and the pair contributions of a subset are never
negative. The suffix budget is still the same triple of sums: future left
sizes, future left pair totals and future right pair totals. At an already
assigned vertex the right size is determined by its left size, so choosing
its actual right neighbours does not alter this budget argument. The
program now subtracts the three actual right pair contributions, whose
sum is exactly the binomial of that determined size. Missing a suffix
triple precludes any completion.

For every proposed literal edge, the current graph is a subgraph of every
possible completion. A path of length at most four between its endpoints
would persist in a completion and create a prohibited short cycle. Therefore
applying this same necessary test earlier, after both sides of a vertex
are chosen, is safe. The terminal graph and the path-of-length-five test
are unchanged. This establishes equality of the sets of complete and
positive labelled assignments produced by the two traversals, not merely
equality of an isomorphism count.

The code uses the same forest and normalization routines and suffix sets.
The independent colour-orbit control and degree-budget checks remain
applicable. A new control compares complete counts and literal positive
adjacency arrays on twelve root relabellings of a known positive graph for
each of the four forest types, using both actual traversal implementations.
No target result or performance factor is inferred from this control.
